{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>No manner of laborious work</b> — even the labors that are important to you, [classified as] 'laborious work' (עֲבוֹדָה) and necessity, such that there is monetary loss in their neglect, such as an irreparable loss (דָּבָר הָאָבֵד). Thus I understood from the Tosefta (chapter 12, halakha 8), which teaches: 'One might say that even the intermediate days of the Festival (חֻלּוֹ שֶׁל מוֹעֵד) are prohibited in laborious work,' and so forth. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 23:8). And it is not correct at all, for what reason would the verse say 'You shall not do laborious work' [specifically] for an irreparable loss, while all other labors would be derived by an a fortiori argument (קַל וָחוֹמֶר)? If so, it would be fitting for it to say the same regarding the Sabbath. Furthermore, if so, then the intermediate days of the Festival are hinted at in the Torah as being permitted for irreparable loss — yet they said (Hagigah 18a): 'Scripture did not hand them over except to the Sages,' for there is no hint in the Torah indicating which labor is permitted and which labor is forbidden. And the term 'עֲבוֹדָה' is inclusive of all labors and services: 'he who works (עֹבֵד) his land shall be satisfied with bread' (Proverbs 12:11); 'and your servant shall serve (וְעָבַדְתָּ) for six years' (Deuteronomy 15:18); 'and you shall serve (וְעָבַדְתָּ) your enemies' (ibid. 28:48). And it does not appear anywhere exclusively in connection with irreparable loss — [as if to say] that if you do not do it today, you will not do it tomorrow. Rather, the meaning of 'laborious work' (מְלֶאכֶת עֲבוֹדָה) is: any labor that is not for the preparation of food (אֹכֶל נֶפֶשׁ), as in: 'Six days you shall labor (תַּעֲבֹד) and do all your work' (Exodus 20:9); 'and all your labor (עֲבוֹדָה) in the field' (ibid. 1:14); 'and you shall work (וְנֶעֱבַדְתֶּם) and you shall sow' (Ezekiel 36:9); 'and Cain was a worker (עֹבֵד) of the ground' (Genesis 4:2). And a labor that is for the preparation of food is labor for enjoyment (מְלֶאכֶת הֲנָאָה), not laborious work. And this is elucidated in the Torah, for regarding the Festival of Unleavened Bread, where it stated at the outset: 'No manner of work shall be done in them' (Exodus 12:16), it was necessary to specify 'but that which every person must eat — that alone may be done for you.' And regarding all other Festival days, He shortens and says: 'You shall not do any laborious work' — to prohibit all labor that is not for the preparation of food, and to make known that food preparation is permitted on them. And the verse never says, for any of the other Festival days, 'all labor' (כָּל מְלָאכָה) [in absolute terms], nor does it specify in them the permissibility of food preparation, for 'laborious work' (מְלֶאכֶת עֲבוֹדָה) teaches this. However, in the section of All the Firstborn (Deuteronomy 16:8), regarding the Festival of Unleavened Bread, it says: 'And on the seventh day there shall be a solemn assembly for HaShem your Elohim; you shall not do any work (מְלָאכָה).' The reason is that He had already explicitly permitted food preparation in it, and there was no need to say 'all laborious work' (כָּל מְלֶאכֶת עֲבוֹדָה); and He mentioned 'work' (מְלָאכָה) without qualification, without saying 'all labor' (כָּל מְלָאכָה), as is said regarding the Sabbath (Leviticus 23:3) and Yom HaKippurim (Leviticus 23:28) — for the intent is: you shall not do the work that I have warned you about. And Rabbeinu Chananel wrote: 'All laborious work' — this teaches that He permits only the labor of food preparation, as is written regarding this matter elsewhere: 'And on the first day there shall be a holy convocation for you; no manner of work shall be done in them' — and because it mentions there 'all labor' (כָּל מְלָאכָה), it was necessary to specify 'but that which every person must eat — that alone may be done for you.' The meaning of 'laborious work' (מְלֶאכֶת עֲבוֹדָה): labor that is preserved for the purpose of acquisition, such as sowing and reaping and digging and the like; but food preparation is not laborious work. This is his language.\n\nAnd the baraita in the Tosefta (chapter 12, halakhot 5–8), as it appears in the precise manuscripts, is as follows: '“You shall not do any work” — this comes to teach about the intermediate days of the Festival that they are prohibited in labor. One might say they are prohibited in all laborious work (כָּל מְלֶאכֶת עֲבוֹדָה) — Scripture says: “it” (הוּא). It — [the Festival day] — is prohibited in all laborious work, but the intermediate days of the Festival are not prohibited in all laborious work.' These are the words of Rabbi Yosi HaGlili. Rabbi Akiva says: 'What does Scripture teach when it says: “These are the appointed seasons of HaShem, which you shall proclaim as holy convocations” (Leviticus 23:37)? If it refers to Festival days, Festival days have already been stated. Why then is it said: “These are the appointed seasons of HaShem, which you shall proclaim as holy convocations”? These are the intermediate days of the Festival, which are prohibited in the performance of labor. One might say they are prohibited in all laborious work — Scripture says: “it,”' and so forth. And its explanation is that Rabbi Yosi HaGlili expounds: 'You shall not do... These are the appointed seasons of HaShem' (below, verses 36–37) — to say that Scripture prohibits labor on all the appointed seasons of HaShem. One might say that they are all equal in prohibition and are prohibited in all laborious work, like the first day and the eighth day — so we expound: 'All laborious work you shall not do. These are the appointed seasons of HaShem,' the verse being expounded both in what precedes it and in what precedes that which precedes it. Scripture then qualifies: 'A solemn assembly — it; all laborious work you shall not do' (Leviticus 23:36) — the eighth day is restricted in all laborious work, but the intermediate days of the Festival are not restricted like it in all laborious work, but only in labor (מְלָאכָה) alone, for it said: 'You shall not do' — but Scripture did not mention it, nor did it hint at it at all, and handed it over to the Sages. And according to Rashi's version, it is explained similarly.\n\nAnd this baraita is cited in Gemara Hagigah (18a), where it teaches: '“All laborious work you shall not do” — this teaches about the intermediate days of the Festival that they are prohibited in the performance of labor.' These are the words of Rabbi Yosi HaGlili. Rabbi Akiva says, and so forth. And there they further taught: 'Just as the seventh day is prohibited, so too the sixth day is prohibited. Now if [you argue] from the fact that the seventh day is restricted in all labor (כָּל מְלָאכָה), should the sixth day also be restricted in all labor? Scripture says: “the seventh” (הַשְּׁבִיעִי) — the seventh is restricted in all labor, but the sixth is not restricted in all labor,' for Scripture handed them over only to the Sages, and so forth. They taught in the Gemara 'all labor' (כָּל מְלָאכָה) and they taught in the Tosefta 'all laborious work' (כָּל מְלֶכֶת עֲבוֹדָה) — they adopted for themselves in the midrash the language of the verse, which does not speak of a Festival day in terms of 'all labor' but rather in terms of 'all laborious work.' Both of them intended the same thing: a Festival day (שְׁיוֹ\"ט) is prohibited in all laborious work, and the intermediate days of the Festival are not prohibited in all laborious work, but they are prohibited in labor (מְלָאכָה) that was not specified in the Torah — and Scripture handed it over to the Sages.\n\nAnd in the Sifrei (Re'eh 135), Rabbi Yishmael says: 'Since we have not learned that the intermediate days of the Festival are prohibited in labor, Scripture says: 'Six days you shall eat unleavened bread, and on the seventh day there shall be a solemn assembly' (Deuteronomy 16:8) — just as the seventh is restricted, so too the sixth is restricted. Now if [you argue] from the fact that the seventh is restricted in all labor, should the sixth also be restricted in all labor? Scripture says: 'the seventh' — the seventh is restricted in all labor, but the sixth is not restricted in all labor.' And this too is according to the approach we have stated.\n\nWe have gone at length concerning the baraitot cited on this matter, in order to present the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory together with the clear and correct interpretation of what we have said regarding these verses.\n\nYet despite all this, I found an objection to our position from a baraita cited in the Sifrei (Pinchas 147): '“All laborious work you shall not do” (Numbers 28:18) — this teaches that it is prohibited in the performance of labor. From where do we derive permission for food preparation? It says here 'holy convocation' (מִקְרָא קֹדֶשׁ) and it says below 'holy convocation' (Exodus 12:16) — just as below it permits food preparation, so too here it permits food preparation.' And it appears to me that 'עֲבוֹדָה' according to our Rabbis of blessed memory means toil and exertion that a person performs for another, from the language of 'the service of a slave' (עֲבוֹדַת עָבֶד, Leviticus 25:39), 'a slave of slaves shall he be to his brothers' (Genesis 9:25), 'they served Chedorlaomer' (Genesis 14:4), and likewise 'the service of labor and the service of burden' (עֲבֹדַת עֲבֹדָה וַעֲבֹדַת מַשָּׂא, Numbers 4:47) — the service of all toil in the Tent and the service of burden on the shoulder. And if so, it would have been possible that light labors that a person performs for his own enjoyment are permitted even though they are not for food preparation, and that abundant food preparation, in which a servant toils for his master, would be prohibited. Therefore it asks: from where do we know that this permission extends to food preparation — such that we permit all food preparation even with toil, and prohibit all other labors even those that are light among them, which are called 'laborious work' (מְלֶאכֶת עֲבוֹדָה)? Scripture says: 'holy convocation' (מִקְרָא קֹדֶשׁ) — [a] gezeirah shavah (analogy), for there all labor comes for prohibition and all food preparation comes for permission.\n\nAnd I saw in the Mekhilta (Bo 9) that it teaches: '“But that which every person must eat” (Exodus 12:16) — all food preparation overrides the Festival day (יוֹם טוֹב), but all laborious work (עֲבוֹדָה) does not override the Festival day. And let some [i.e., a minimal amount of] food preparation override the Sabbath — and logic dictates: if in a case where all laborious work does not override the Festival day, all food preparation does override the Festival day, then in a case where some labor overrides the Sabbath, is it not logical that some food preparation should override the Sabbath? Scripture says: 'that every person must eat' — some labor overrides the Sabbath but some food preparation does not override the Sabbath.' And the meaning of 'some labor' (מִקְצָת עֲבוֹדָה) is the obligation of the day — the daily offerings and the additional offerings. And 'all laborious work does not override the Festival day' refers to vows and freewill offerings or a freewill burnt offering. But 'some food preparation' (מִקְצָת אֹכֶל נֶפֶשׁ) — its meaning has not become clear to me, except that it may hint at what we have said: that which involves great toil, being comparable to labor, falls under the category of prohibition, while that which is done lightly, in the manner of a person for himself, is permitted — or 'some of it' means [sufficient] for his livelihood, while 'all of it' means abundant cooked dishes for indulgence. And the general principle in 'laborious work' (מְלֶאכֶת עֲבוֹדָה) is that it serves to permit food preparation (אֹכֶל נֶפֶשׁ)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The abbreviation שיו\"ט in the manuscript is resolved as שְׁנֵיהֶם לְדָבָר אֶחָד נִתְכַּוְּנוּ יוֹם טוֹב based on context; it is also possible it stands simply for יוֹם טוֹב. The Tosefta chapter/halakha citations follow the numbering in standard editions; Ramban's manuscript may reflect a different arrangement."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:24:55.085555441Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}